<PageLayout title='Paleogenomics & Deep-Time Biology' kicker="RESEARCH" lead='Genomes can preserve histories that no living observation can recover directly.'>
<section><div class="prose content-page"><p>Ancient DNA adds time to population genomics. Instead of inferring every event from living descendants, temporally sampled genomes can provide direct snapshots of populations that no longer exist.</p><p>This research interest grows from experience with ancient DNA and paleogenomic questions, including human and archaeological contexts, but the broader goal is methodological: combine genomic evidence with chronology, archaeology, fossils and evolutionary models.</p><h2>$ questions</h2><ul><li>How do extinct populations relate to living lineages?</li><li>Which demographic histories become identifiable when time-structured samples are available?</li><li>How can genomic, archaeological and fossil evidence constrain one another?</li><li>How should uncertainty and damage be propagated through downstream inference?</li></ul></div></section>
</PageLayout>